///sha-256;sBrhthg_K297KXGo53Mv_ErESbIoUByyl_ZcEJPSSN8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2026-07-20T23:57:20.601Zƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;uOT2EjJVgvTZk9xRvbzEFyaRGmAScAHvGRU8U2FEkMAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx|https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/jul/20/yellow-pine-by-claire-vaye-watkins-review-messy-portrait-of-protest-subcultureƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;8HOmbvgzWoCdKHs8H6JaAOQDihElnj0-pTi72JbI7igƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9https://newscable-source-broker.invalid/activitypub/actorƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;4SwYGq3p8k5ny9nZ2-RqrD6V8hynrLnpyyrarkwgUqoƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Nj1V2-tIIvPNNn9Ie1asBuazn4nGp3LxZ-fhmY6eIQQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx|https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/jul/20/yellow-pine-by-claire-vaye-watkins-review-messy-portrait-of-protest-subcultureƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ftonDwlKo0a3l-zcA2eoF-PDNE7PtyH3kcwD5GDFtEoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky<p>Watkins should have been the ideal writer for this sympathetic take on new age spirituality and desert eco-activism â€“ but the results are confusing</p><p>Rose, the protagonist of Claire Vaye Watkinsâ€™s latest novel, has retreated from the world to live in Nothingness Flats, a cinder-block cabin in the Nevada desert. On indefinite leave from her job at an environmental organisation, Rose works on her Al-Anon recovery, goes hiking and joins protests against the Yellow Pine of the title: a solar array being built over a fragile desert ecosystem. She writes in her journal: â€œI am on this side of the portal. I have compassion in my heart for anyone back on the other side, sending emails, bickering, buying useless shit, getting run by the algorithm, living in the past.â€ As the story begins, she is reunited with her first love, Miles, who has â€œfallen apart beautifully: bopped in and out of law school, then spent six years in whatâ€™s left of the redwoods reading horny 400-year-old poems before bailing on his PhDâ€. Soon, Rose realises what she truly wants, this side of the portal, is to have his child.</p><p>You donâ€™t often see a sympathetic take on the subculture of 21st-century hippies, with their eclectic leftwing philosophies, new age spirituality and earnest striving to do good and be kind. Itâ€™s an important strain in modern life, and one typically dismissed with facile mockery and condescension. As a serious treatment of it, Yellow Pine has notable strengths. The love story between Rose and Miles is very real and specific to this world, and gives a&nbsp;backbone of plot to material that otherwise tends to sprawl. Watkinsâ€™s prose can be musical and keenly attentive, and she excels at conveying starry-eyed love. â€œ[Miles] was brilliant, peaceful, grounded as a mountain, kind even when he was blue, an unabashed loner agog at the whole burning world.â€</p> <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/jul/20/yellow-pine-by-claire-vaye-watkins-review-messy-portrait-of-protest-subculture">Continue reading...</a>ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;dn1kSKwDKkq1yzOycF70LgccRLPDXq23BHzeUY8v0GYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;jI1YOsc7XuWK2UOmQs38AsXdqKiIZyjeWQcbRSEjzFcƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;KxL81OTA47mYY2tyaE6E3c0Vi8vhaNKsS-DgZeUlGEcƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEnti